# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Translation, paragraph for paragraph. Index = paragraph number in paras_ru.json. <<X>> marks a speaker change (N = narrator); markers are removed from translation_en.json.
EN = {}
EN[0] = "The last day before Christmas had passed. A clear winter night had come. The stars looked out. The moon rose majestically into the sky to shed light on good people and on the whole world, so that everyone might have a merry time singing carols and praising Christ. The frost was sharper than in the morning, but it was so still that the creak of the frost under a boot could be heard half a mile away. Not one crowd of lads had yet appeared under the windows of the cottages; only the moon peeped into them, stealthily, as if calling the girls who were dressing up to run out quickly onto the creaking snow. Then smoke came tumbling in clouds out of the chimney of one cottage and spread like a storm cloud across the sky, and with the smoke a witch rose up, riding on a broom."
EN[1] = "If at that moment the Sorochintsy assessor had been driving by in a troika of requisitioned horses, in a cap with a lambskin band cut in the lancer fashion, in a blue sheepskin coat lined with black astrakhan, with the devilishly plaited whip he is accustomed to use on his driver, he would certainly have noticed her, because no witch in the world can slip past the Sorochintsy assessor. He knows to the last piglet how many a woman's sow has farrowed, and how much linen lies in her chest, and exactly which of his clothes and belongings a good man will pawn at the tavern on Sunday. But the Sorochintsy assessor was not driving by, and why should he care about other people's affairs? He has his own district. Meanwhile the witch rose so high that she showed only as a little black speck up there. But wherever the speck appeared, the stars vanished from the sky, one after another. Soon the witch had gathered a whole sleeveful of them. Three or four were still shining. Suddenly, from the other side, another speck appeared, grew bigger, began to stretch out, and was a speck no longer. A short-sighted man, even if he put the wheels of a commissary's carriage on his nose instead of spectacles, would not have made out what it was. In front he was a perfect German: a narrow little muzzle, forever turning and sniffing at everything that came its way, ended, like our pigs', in a round little snout; his legs were so thin that if the Yareski headman had had such legs he would have broken them in the first Cossack dance. But behind he was a real provincial attorney in uniform, because a tail hung from him, as sharp and long as the tails of a uniform coat nowadays; only by the goat's beard under his muzzle, by the little horns sticking out of his head, and by being no whiter than a chimney sweep could one guess that he was neither a German nor a provincial attorney, but simply a devil, who had one last night left to wander the wide world and teach good people their sins. Tomorrow, at the first bells for morning service, he would run without looking back, tail between his legs, to his lair. Meanwhile the devil crept quietly up to the moon and had already stretched out his hand to seize it; but suddenly he snatched it back, as if burned, sucked his fingers, jigged about on one foot, ran round to the other side, and again jumped away and drew back his hand. Still, in spite of all his failures, the cunning devil did not give up his mischief. He ran up and suddenly grabbed the moon with both hands, and, grimacing and blowing, tossed it from one hand to the other, like a peasant who has picked up a live coal with his bare hands to light his pipe; at last he hurriedly hid it in his pocket and, as if nothing had happened, ran on. In Dikanka no one heard how the devil stole the moon. True, the district clerk, coming out of the tavern on all fours, saw the moon dancing in the sky for no reason at all, and swore to the whole village that it was so; but the villagers shook their heads and even laughed at him. And what was the reason that made the devil decide on such a lawless deed? This was the reason: he knew that the rich Cossack Chub had been invited by the deacon to the Christmas Eve supper of kutya, where the headman would be; and the deacon's kinsman, who had come from the bishop's choir, in a blue frock coat, singing the lowest bass; and the Cossack Svyerbyhuz and a few others; where, besides the kutya, there would be varenukha, vodka brewed with saffron, and plenty of other good things to eat. Meanwhile Chub's daughter, the beauty of the whole village, would stay at home, and to the daughter the blacksmith would surely come, a strong man and a fine fellow, whom the devil hated worse than the sermons of Father Kondrat. In his spare time from his work the blacksmith took up painting, and was known as the best painter in all the neighbourhood. Colonel L...ko himself, who was still alive then, sent for him specially to Poltava to paint the board fence round his house. All the bowls from which the Dikanka Cossacks ate their borscht had been painted by the blacksmith. The blacksmith was a God-fearing man and often painted icons of the saints, and even now you can find his Evangelist Luke in the church at T... But the triumph of his art was a picture painted on the church wall in the right-hand porch, in which he had shown Saint Peter on the Day of Judgement, with the keys in his hands, driving the evil spirit out of hell: the frightened devil threw himself in every direction, sensing his doom, while the sinners who had been shut up there before beat and drove him with whips, logs and anything that came to hand. While the painter was working on this picture, painting it on a large wooden board, the devil tried with all his might to hinder him: he jogged his arm unseen, lifted ashes from the forge's furnace and sprinkled them over the picture; but in spite of everything the work was finished, the board was carried into the church and set into the wall of the porch, and from that time the devil swore to take revenge on the blacksmith. He had only one night left to wander the world; but even on this night he was looking for some way to pay the blacksmith back for his spite. And for that he decided to steal the moon, hoping that old Chub was lazy and slow to get going, and that the deacon's house was not close to his cottage: the road ran behind the village, past the windmills, past the cemetery, and wound round a ravine. Even on a moonlit night the varenukha and the vodka brewed with saffron might have lured Chub; but in such darkness it would hardly have been possible for anyone to drag him off his stove and out of his cottage. And the blacksmith, who had long been on bad terms with him, would never dare, with Chub there, to go to the daughter, strong as he was. And so, as soon as the devil had hidden his moon in his pocket, it suddenly became so dark all over the world that not everyone could have found the way to the tavern, let alone to the deacon's. The witch, finding herself suddenly in the dark, cried out. Then the devil, sidling up like a little imp, took her by the arm and began whispering in her ear the very thing that is usually whispered to the whole female race. A strange arrangement we have in this world! Everything that lives in it tries to copy and mimic everything else. Once upon a time, in Mirgorod, only the judge and the mayor went about in winter in sheepskin coats covered with cloth, while all the petty officials wore plain bare ones; but now the assessor and the chamberlain have both had new fur coats made from Reshetilovka lambskin with cloth covers. The clerk and the district scribe, the year before last, bought blue Chinese cotton at sixty kopecks an arshin. The sexton has made himself nankeen trousers for summer and a waistcoat of striped cloth. In a word, everyone is climbing up in the world! When will these people stop being vain? One could wager that many will find it surprising to see the devil setting off down the same road. What is most annoying is that he surely imagines himself a handsome fellow, while his figure is a shame to look at. A face, as Foma Grigoryevich says, the ugliest of the ugly, and yet even he courts the ladies! But in the sky and under the sky it had become so dark that it was no longer possible to see what happened next between them."
EN[3] = "<<CHUB>>“So you, friend, have not been to the deacon's new cottage yet?”<<N>> said the Cossack Chub, coming out of the door of his cottage, to a lean, tall peasant in a short sheepskin coat, with an overgrown beard, which showed that for more than two weeks it had not been touched by the bit of scythe blade with which peasants usually shave for want of a razor. <<CHUB>>“There'll be a fine drinking party there now,”<<N>> Chub went on, his face breaking into a grin. <<CHUB>>“If only we are not late.”<<N>> With this Chub straightened his belt, which held his sheepskin coat tight, pulled his cap down more firmly, gripped in his hand his whip, the terror and scourge of pestering dogs, and then, glancing upward, stopped short. <<CHUB>>“What the devil! Look! Look, Panas!”"
EN[4] = "<<PANAS>>“What?”<<N>> said his friend, and lifted his head, looking up too."
EN[5] = "<<CHUB>>“What do you mean, what? There's no moon!”"
EN[6] = "<<PANAS>>“What the plague! There really is no moon.”"
EN[7] = "<<CHUB>>“That's just it, there isn't,”<<N>> said Chub, with some annoyance at his friend's unfailing calm. <<CHUB>>“You, I dare say, don't care.”"
EN[8] = "<<PANAS>>“What am I to do about it!”"
EN[9] = "<<CHUB>>“It had to be some devil,”<<N>> Chub went on, wiping his moustache with his sleeve, <<CHUB>>“may that dog never get a glass of vodka in the morning, to meddle in this! Really, as if to mock us. I sat in the cottage on purpose and looked out of the window: the night was a marvel! Bright; the snow shone in the moonlight. You could see everything as in the day. I had hardly stepped out of the door when, look, you can't see your hand before your face!”<<N>> Chub went on grumbling and swearing for a long time, and meanwhile was wondering what to decide. He was dying to chat about all sorts of nonsense at the deacon's, where without a doubt the headman was already sitting, and the visiting bass, and Mykyta the tar-seller, who drove to Poltava every fortnight to the markets and let fly such jokes that all the villagers held their sides with laughter. Chub could already see in his mind the varenukha standing on the table. All this was tempting, it was true; but the darkness of the night reminded him of that laziness which is so dear to every Cossack. How good it would be now to lie with his legs tucked under him on the stove-bench, calmly smoking his pipe, and through a blissful drowsiness listen to the carols and songs of the merry lads and girls crowding in bunches under the windows. Without a doubt he would have decided on the latter if he had been alone; but now, with two of them, it was not so dull or frightening to go in the dark night, and he also did not want to appear lazy or cowardly in front of others. When he had finished his curses, he turned again to his friend."
EN[10] = "<<CHUB>>“So there's no moon, friend?”"
EN[11] = "<<PANAS>>“No.”"
EN[12] = "<<CHUB>>“Strange, really. And give me a pinch of snuff! You've got famous snuff, friend! Where do you get it?”"
EN[13] = "<<PANAS>>“Famous, what the devil!”<<N>> answered his friend, closing his birch snuffbox, pricked all over with patterns. <<PANAS>>“An old hen wouldn't sneeze at it!”"
EN[14] = "<<CHUB>>“I remember,”<<N>> Chub went on in the same tone, <<CHUB>>“the late tavern-keeper Zuzulya once brought me tobacco from Nezhin. Ah, that was tobacco! Good tobacco! Well, friend, what shall we do? It's dark out.”"
EN[15] = "<<PANAS>>“Then perhaps we should stay at home,”<<N>> said his friend, taking hold of the door handle."
EN[16] = "If his friend had not said that, Chub would surely have decided to stay; but now something seemed to be pulling him to go against him. <<CHUB>>“No, friend, let's go! We can't not, we must go!”<<N>> No sooner had he said this than he was annoyed with himself for saying it. It was most unpleasant to him to trudge out on such a night; but it consoled him that he had wanted it himself, on purpose, and had done as he pleased and not as he had been advised."
EN[17] = "The friend, without showing the slightest trace of annoyance on his face, like a man to whom it is quite the same whether he sits at home or trudges out of it, looked about, scratched his shoulders with the stick of his whip, and the two friends set off."
EN[19] = "Now let us see what the beautiful daughter is doing, left alone. Oksana was not yet seventeen, and all over the world, one might say, on this side of Dikanka and on that, there was talk of nothing but her. The lads declared in chorus that there had never yet been a better girl in the village, and never would be. Oksana knew and heard everything that was said about her, and was capricious, as a beauty is. If she had gone about not in a plakhta and a zapaska but in some kind of fine town gown, she would have driven away all her girl friends. Lads chased after her in crowds, but, losing patience, they left her little by little and turned to others who were not so spoiled. Only the blacksmith was stubborn and did not give up his courting, although he too was treated no better than the rest. After her father had gone out, she went on dressing up and primping for a long time before a small mirror in a pewter frame, and could not admire herself enough. <<OKSANA>>“What have people taken it into their heads to spread about, that I am pretty?”<<N>> she said, as if absent-mindedly, only to have something to chatter about with herself. <<OKSANA>>“People lie, I am not pretty at all.”<<N>> But the fresh, lively face that flashed in the mirror, young as a child's, with shining black eyes and an inexpressibly pleasant smile that burned into the soul, suddenly proved the opposite. <<OKSANA>>“Are my black brows and eyes,”<<N>> the beauty went on, not letting go of the mirror, <<OKSANA>>“so lovely that there is nothing like them in the world? What is good about this turned-up nose? And these cheeks? And these lips? Are my black braids lovely, then? Oh! They could frighten you in the evening: like long snakes, they are twisted and wound around my head. I see now that I am not pretty at all!”<<N>> And, holding the mirror a little farther from her, she cried out: <<OKSANA>>“No, I am pretty! Oh, how pretty! A wonder! What joy I shall bring to him whose wife I become! How my husband will admire me! He won't remember himself. He will kiss me to death!”"
EN[20] = "<<VAKULA>>“A strange girl!”<<N>> whispered the blacksmith, who had come in quietly. <<VAKULA>>“And she isn't short of boasting! She has been standing an hour looking in the mirror, and cannot look enough, and even praises herself aloud!”"
EN[21] = "<<OKSANA>>“Yes, lads, are you a match for me? Look at me,”<<N>> the pretty coquette went on, <<OKSANA>>“how smoothly I walk; my shift is sewn with red silk. And what ribbons on my head! You will never see richer braid! My father bought me all this so that the finest young man in the world would marry me!”<<N>> And, smiling, she turned the other way, and saw the blacksmith..."
EN[22] = "She cried out and stopped sternly in front of him."
EN[23] = "The blacksmith let his arms drop."
EN[24] = "It is hard to say what the dark-skinned face of the wonderful girl expressed: there was sternness in it, and through the sternness some mockery of the confused blacksmith, and a barely noticeable flush of vexation spread delicately over her face; and all this was so mingled and so indescribably lovely that to kiss her a million times was the best thing anyone could have done then."
EN[25] = "<<OKSANA>>“Why have you come here?”<<N>> Oksana began. <<OKSANA>>“Do you want me to drive you out of the door with a shovel? You are all masters at getting round us. You sniff out in a moment when our fathers are not at home. Oh, I know you! Well, is my chest ready?”"
EN[26] = "<<VAKULA>>“It will be ready, my little heart, it will be ready after the holiday. If you knew how much I have slaved over it: two nights I did not leave the forge; but not one priest's daughter will have such a chest. I put on it iron for the binding such as I did not put on the colonel's cart when I went to work in Poltava. And how it will be painted! Though you walk the whole district on your little white feet, you will not find one like it! Red and blue flowers will be scattered all over the field. It will burn like fire. Don't be angry with me! Let me at least talk, at least look at you!”"
EN[27] = "<<OKSANA>>“Who forbids you? Talk and look!”<<N>> She sat down on the bench and again looked in the mirror, and began to set her braids straight on her head. She looked at her neck, at her new shift embroidered with silk, and a delicate feeling of self-satisfaction showed on her lips, on her fresh cheeks, and shone in her eyes."
EN[28] = "<<VAKULA>>“Let me sit beside you, too!”<<N>> said the blacksmith."
EN[29] = "<<OKSANA>>“Sit,”<<N>> said Oksana, keeping the same feeling on her lips and in her contented eyes."
EN[30] = "<<VAKULA>>“Wonderful, dearest Oksana, let me kiss you!”<<N>> said the emboldened blacksmith, and pressed her to him, meaning to snatch a kiss; but Oksana turned away her cheeks, which were already at an imperceptible distance from the blacksmith's lips, and pushed him off. <<OKSANA>>“What more do you want? When he has honey, he needs a spoon too! Go away, your hands are harder than iron. And you smell of smoke yourself. I think you have smeared me all over with soot.”<<N>> Then she held up the mirror and began to preen before it again."
EN[31] = "<<VAKULA>>“She doesn't love me!”<<N>> the blacksmith thought to himself, hanging his head. <<VAKULA>>“To her it is all a game; and I stand before her like a fool and can't take my eyes off her. And I would go on standing before her, and never take my eyes off her! A wonderful girl! What would I not give to know what is in her heart, whom she loves. But no, she does not care about anyone. She admires herself; she torments me, poor man; and for sorrow I can't see the light; and I love her as no man in the world has loved, or ever will love.”"
EN[32] = "<<OKSANA>>“Is it true that your mother is a witch?”<<N>> said Oksana, and laughed; and the blacksmith felt that everything inside him laughed. That laughter seemed at once to echo in his heart and in his gently trembling veins, and yet vexation sank into his soul that it was not in his power to kiss that face which had laughed so sweetly."
EN[33] = "<<VAKULA>>“What do I care about my mother? You are mother to me, and father, and everything that is dear in the world. If the tsar called me and said: Blacksmith Vakula, ask of me whatever is best in my kingdom, and I will give it to you. I shall order a golden forge to be made for you, and you will forge with silver hammers. I don't want, I would say to the tsar, neither precious stones, nor a golden forge, nor all your kingdom: give me rather my Oksana!”"
EN[34] = "<<OKSANA>>“See what you are! But my father is no fool himself. You will see whether he marries your mother!”<<N>> said Oksana, smiling slyly. <<OKSANA>>“But the girls are not coming... What can it mean? It is high time to go carolling. I am getting bored.”"
EN[35] = "<<VAKULA>>“God be with them, my beauty!”"
EN[36] = "<<OKSANA>>“Not likely! The lads will surely come with them. Then the fun begins. I can imagine what funny stories they will tell!”"
EN[37] = "<<VAKULA>>“So you have a good time with them?”"
EN[38] = "<<OKSANA>>“Yes, better than with you. Ah! Someone knocked; it must be the girls with the lads.”"
EN[39] = "<<VAKULA>>“What more is there to wait for?”<<N>> the blacksmith said to himself. <<VAKULA>>“She is mocking me. To her I am as dear as a rusted horseshoe. But if so, then at least no one else will get to laugh at me. Let me only see for certain who pleases her more than I do; I will cure him...”"
EN[40] = "A knock at the door and a voice ringing sharply in the frost, “Open!”, interrupted his thoughts."
EN[41] = "<<VAKULA>>“Wait, I will open it myself,”<<N>> said the blacksmith, and went out into the passage, meaning in his vexation to break the ribs of the first man he met."
